      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Experiments on conscious rats studied the effects of systemic or central application of a nitric oxide (NO) donor or NO-synthase inhibitor on the pain sensitivity threshold. Injection of SNAP in doses of 0.2, 2.0, and 20.0 micrograms into the subarachnoidal space of the ventral medulla through a preimplanted steel cannula was accompanied by a dose-dependent change in tail-flick latency. Intraperitoneal injection of 30 mg L-NAME also increased pain sensitivity threshold levels. The findings suggest that the decrease in pain sensitivity after systemic NO-synthase inhibitor administration is due to the modulation of NO-dependent processes at both the central and peripheral levels."},
